--TEST--
`Core\Request::queryAs` and `postAs` read the whole parameter set as the type written at the call site, converting each field with `as` and leaving every other key behind — `rule:types/arrays`, `rule:types/shape-type` and `rule:types/conversion`
--GET--
page=2&q=novis
--POST--
age=41&name=ada&csrf=t0ken
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Request::queryAs
// covers: Core\Request::postAs
// `rule:types/arrays`'s type-argument door over a request: with no `name`
// option the subject is the whole parameter set, which is the only way a
// program reaches it — neither member opens a public `query(): array<mixed>`.
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Request::queryAs<tainted {page: uint, q: string}>()), "\n";

// `rule:types/conversion`'s `string` → `int` row, and nothing new beside it: a
// form's fields are all text, so a declared `int` asks for exactly the
// whole-string reading `as int` performs.
//
// `rule:types/shape-type`'s width subtyping is the other half of the line: the
// `csrf` key the shape does not name is left behind rather than refused, so a
// form carrying a token does not break a handler reading two fields out of it.
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Request::postAs<tainted {age: int, name: string}>()), "\n";
--EXPECT--
{"page":2,"q":"novis"}
{"age":41,"name":"ada"}
